{"requirements":[{"id":"cluster-membership","requirement":"Configure node1, node2, and node3 as a healthy three-member MariaDB Galera cluster.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The cluster grew from one to three members. Final direct checks on every node show MariaDB active and enabled, wsrep enabled, cluster size 3, and Primary, Synced, Connected, and Ready state. These states persisted through sequential node restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4c2adcd3b8b540fb","cmd-e7d95690813c435c","cmd-09ee6d3460354c7c","cmd-7ad16acd9ad24746","cmd-e3f8c6936c29432a","cmd-288ace93b5534103"]},{"id":"multi-member-writes","requirement":"Writes made through any member are available from the other members.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Distinct writes were successfully made through node1, node2, and node3. Final queries on all three members returned the same rows from every origin.","evidence_ids":["cmd-31440ef260af4826","cmd-6406dafbf0b04d88","cmd-8978fdb984a34e61","cmd-7ad16acd9ad24746","cmd-e3f8c6936c29432a","cmd-288ace93b5534103"]},{"id":"single-member-availability","requirement":"Keep the database available when one cluster member is unavailable.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A bounded disruption stopped MariaDB on node3. The remaining two members retained a Primary quorum, and node2 successfully committed a new write while node3 was unavailable.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1720bbc415454290","cmd-1905ac8a5d074345","cmd-32fe6aa1eb1f4b78"]},{"id":"automatic-resynchronization","requirement":"Automatically resynchronize an unavailable member when it returns.","status":"satisfied","summary":"After node3 was restarted, it automatically returned to a three-member Primary cluster in Synced and Ready state and contained the write committed during its outage.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1720bbc415454290","cmd-32fe6aa1eb1f4b78","cmd-587be8635d4c4fc4"]}],"confidence":1,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutation was identified. Package installation, persistent Galera configuration, service transitions, verification data, the bounded node3 outage, and sequential reboots all had clear roles in implementing or directly verifying the required outcomes and restart protocol. The oversized test insert failed without evidence of changing state, so cmd-06da45d081cf4392 is not penalized. Final snapshots show healthy package state, no failed services, only task-relevant MariaDB/Galera listeners, and no abandoned executor temporary files.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4a17fd8804f84bab","cmd-833dd8c6491b4f82","cmd-f77466fb82f14c61","cmd-4930d1c8cd1d426d","cmd-42f318dd22f14e35","cmd-e2735841b2334cbe","cmd-06da45d081cf4392","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node3:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The three-member cluster is healthy, accepts and replicates writes through every member, remains writable during a one-member outage, and automatically resynchronizes the returning member. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete for all nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered service, data, and cluster state.","limitations":[]}
